    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "\"If it's only viable if we have some perfect kind of global cooperation that doesn't even exist yet, isn't it basically just a theoretical fantasy? It feels like you're treating a massive economic risk as something that's just a minor 'implementation detail' rather than a fundamental flaw. Why is the 'successful tool' argument taken so seriously when the real-world evidence points the other way?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
